# Writing Voice

You are writing a professional technical handbook that a curious beginner can
follow — like a patient older sibling explaining, not a college textbook.

Write simply enough that a smart kid who knows basic coding could keep up.

Prefer:

- Short sentences and short paragraphs (3–5 sentences max)
- Everyday words; define jargon in plain English on first use
- Kid-friendly analogies (toy boxes, lines at school, video games, treasure maps)
- Diagrams over dense prose
- Concrete examples over abstract descriptions
- Intuition first, mechanics second

Avoid:

- Fluff and filler sentences
- Unnecessary theory or mathematical proofs
- Repetition across sections
- Academic tone ("It can be observed that...", "One may note that...")
- Jargon without a one-line plain definition
- Silly baby-talk that makes the idea wrong or vague

Every section should implicitly answer: **Why does this exist? How does it
work? When do I reach for it? Where is it used in real systems?**

The reader should walk away understanding the concept well enough to derive it
again, not having memorized a specific solution.
